most of us think of Saudi Arabia as the  oil Kingdom they're one of the most  dominant voices in the industry and they  profit hundreds of billions of dollars  every single year from pumping out oil  but something that many of us Overlook  is that Saudi Arabia is also the Islamic  Kingdom Saudi Arabia is home to Mecca  which is said to be the holiest of  Islamic cities the founder of Islam  Muhammad the prophet was not only born  in Mecca but this is also where he had  his Revelations way back in the early  7th Century given this religious  significance I don't think you'd be  surprised to hear that most Muslims try  to pilgrimage to this holy city at least  once in their lifetimes this is what  leads to an influx of millions of  pilgrims to the country on an annual  basis for the longest time the only way  you could even get into Saudi Arabia was  to either be a pilgrim or to have a work  visa it wasn't till September of 2019  that Saudi Arabia started accepting  tourists but even today Mecca has  restricted to only Muslims and trying to  sneak in like Iraq is taking extremely  seriously hearing all this you would  think that Saudi Arabia would also be  super keen on protecting the city's  cultural and the architectural history  but this is actually not the case in  fact Saudi Arabia has been actively  destroying Mecca's architecture for  decades since 1985 Saudi Arabia has  destroyed 98 of their historical and  religious sites the vast majority of  which was in Mecca if this number was 5  or 10 you may be able to argue that  there's no agenda but at 98 there's  clearly something going on here one  researcher puts it as it's as if they  wanted to wipe out history and it ends  not like they're always building new  religious sites on top of these ruins  either more times than not they're  building hotels Resorts and sometimes  it's a lot worse for example they  destroy the house of the prophet's first  wife in order to build public toilets I  mean at this point they're just being  insulting so why in the world is Saudi  Arabia destroying their cultural  heritage  taking a look back the destruction of  Mecca is not a new phenomenon by any  means in fact it has been an ongoing  trend for nearly a thousand years dating  as far back as 1205. while it's been a  long time since then the reasoning  behind current destruction is not that  different it all comes down to the  beliefs of one sector of Islam called  wahhabism wahhabism is the prevailing  form of Islam across Saudi Arabia and  they're not fans of worshiping people  visiting the shrines and tombs of even  prophets and Saints is heavily frowned  upon it's thought that visiting such  places will result in Islam's gravest  Senate which is to worship anyone other  than God this does make sense to a  degree if millions of people visit the  homes and tombs of religious figures  it's only natural that some of them  start to see these religious figures as  God themselves but while that is a risk  it does seem rather harsh to wipe out  centuries worth of Heritage just to  prevent a few from quote unquote going  out of line the way that Saudi Arabia  justifies this is by building structures  that support their view of Islam just  because Saudi Arabia has been destroying  most of Mecca does not mean that they're  looking to destroy all of Mecca when it  comes to sites that they believe are  truly pure and holy like the Kaaba they  not only protect it but they try their  best to enhance it over the years Saudi  Arabia has poured tens of billions of  dollars into the Kaaba and the buildings  that surround it a perfect example of  this is the Glorious clock tower that  they built right next to the Grand  Mosque of Mecca not only does the clock  tower look magnificent but it has  extraordinary capacity the main tower  itself has over 16 million square feet  of floor area and contains 1 618 rooms  hearing this I don't think you'd be  surprised to hear that it cost 15  billion dollars to construct and this is  just one of their many Hajj related  projects they've built several top of  the line hotels parking garages  restaurants and everything else that you  might need to have a positive pilgrimage  they've also spent a substantial time  and resources trying to expand the area  that surrounds the Kaaba this way more  people can visit on an annual basis the  most recent Expansion Project was  completed in 2018 which brought the  total size of the great mosque to 187  Acres capable of supporting 2.5 million  worshipers as you can see the Saudi  royal family is by no means anti-islam  if anything there are actually massive  supporters but the key thing to note is  that they only support their view of  Islam historically they had no issues  just blatantly raiding and destroying  people of different sectors but in  modern times Saudi Arabia's approach to  the situation has become more  sophisticated they're not destroying  these cultural sites just to destroy  them they're destroying them so that  they can build structures that support  wahhabi Islam by Framing it like this  the Saudis have been able to shift Focus  away from their destruction of Heritage  and move it over to their construction  of Heritage and this framing has allowed  them to continue their destruction out  with a substantially less backlash  while the Saudi family likes to  perpetuate that their destruction is  driven by pure and holy religious  reasons only there is little doubt that  keeping power likely also plays a role  in their decisions Saudi Arabia is an  absolute monarchy meaning that they  still have Kings and princes and these  are the guys that rule over the country  they don't have an elected prime  minister or president or anything along  those lines in fact they virtually never  have elections power over the country is  slowly passed down within the royal  family alone history has taught us that  such monarchy that are almost always  overthrown by rebellions and Wars but  Saudi Arabia has actually had a pretty  successful history with the monarchy now  this isn't to say that Saudi Arabia has  been perfect they've had a rather Shady  history when it comes to Human Rights  and women's rights but neither of these  have been enough to spark rebellions  within the country and the key reason  for this is that the kingdom has given  its people the 3Gs God gold and Glory in  terms of God it's clear that they're  supporting the prevailing form of Islam  within the kingdom wahhabism to the  maximum extent most Muslims within the  kingdom are either neutral regarding  this issue or they actually support the  destruction one user commented this is  good we don't worship decorated Graves  this proves it most of the Muslims that  are unhappy with the destruction are  located outside the Kingdom so they  don't present that much of a threat to  this Saudi family's power in terms of  gold Saudi Arabia has literally stacked  their reserves with nearly half a  trillion dollars worth of gold and cash  they also have extremely lenient  Taxation and Welfare policies when it  comes to Saudi Nationals in fact Saudi  Arabia has no personal income tax so  most Saudi Nationals have no complaints  when it comes to the economy and finally  in terms of Glory Saudi Arabia is  literally the most prominent Islamic  country in the world both religiously  and economically when Saudi citizens are  getting God golden and Gloria they have  very little reason to Rebel or question  the power of the Saudi family but  imagine for a second if Saudi Arabia did  not destroy these historical buildings  Not only would they not be appeasing the  Hobbies within the country but they  would simultaneously be creating an  opportunity for non-wahabi beliefs to  grow and prosper within the kingdom this  is clearly not something that they want  given that they try to discredit  historical sites with every opportunity  that they get for example Muhammad the  prophet's apparent birthplace is  surrounded by signs in four languages  that tell visitors that there is no  proof that Muhammad was actually born  there if this Saudis didn't do this  they'd just be asking for non-hobby  sectors to grow it which would not only  create tension between this group and  the government but also between this  group and the rest of Saudi Arabia so it  would essentially be cultivating a  rebellion or a civil war you'll see  people questioning the power of the  Saudi family and you'll probably see  some religious figure rise up and lead  the Revolution and based on history  religion-based conflict is usually the  most dangerous form of conflict given  that both sides have unbreakable beliefs  I don't know about you but I don't think  the Saudi royal family wants to end up  like the Iranian Shah so they likely  won't be stopping the destruction at any  time soon  while the Saudis policy of Destruction  has played out decently well within  Saudi Arabia itself the same cannot be  said about its non-wahabi Muslims to  many of them Saudi Arabia's actions lack  even the basics of decency in the modern  world just because you don't agree with  another person's religion it does not  mean that you get to go destroy their  history and even if these sites aren't  wholly like the saudius claimant they're  at the very least important to their own  history and culture the saudi's decision  to destroy these sites despite this  connection has come off as extremism to  many the common belief amongst these  people is that the Saudi family's ego is  way too high and that they're putting  control on profits over history and  culture after all it's not like they had  to destroy these sites to even build  their hotels in the first place there's  plenty of space on the outskirts of  Mecca where insane hotels and resorts  could have been built instead it's not  just non-wahabi Muslims that are upset  with Saudi Arabia's actions either  historians and archaeologists are also  not a fan of this Behavior either these  historical sites likely would have given  more context as to how people survived  on the peninsula for the past several  Millennia for historians this is not  just a matter of protecting Islamic  history but a matter of human history  many historians and activists have been  posting pictures of Islamic historical  sites on social media to try to raise  awareness surrounding the situation but  this hasn't really done all that much  because no one is going to speak out  against Saudi Arabia Muslim countries  that don't want Saudi Arabia to reduce  their Hajj pilgrimage quotas meanwhile  Western countries that don't want to  jeopardize their source of oil one of  the only exceptions to this is the  former president of religious Affairs of  turkey mehmed Gomez he told the Saudi  minister of the Hajj that the skyscraper  overshadowing the kabad destroys history  but honestly I don't think this Audis  really cared about his thoughts so while  resentment is building outside the king  this probably won't be leading to any  sort of change anytime soon  in the end the destruction of much of  Mecca is truly quite a shame whether  these places are holy or not they're  important piece of human history that  should be studied and appreciated but  instead the Saudis have been slowly  destroying the city for hundreds of  years and this has especially  accelerated within the past few decades  they've Justified their destruction by  claiming that these sites lead to sins  and that they're not in line with the  wahhabi way but while they don't admit  it it seems like keeping control and  power also plays a major role in this  decision while this has angered many  Muslims and non-muslims alike they're  simply not much that anyone can do about  it after all it's not like the Saudis  are going over to Egypt and destroying  their pyramids the Saudis are simply  destroying their own buildings so you  can't do much more than just give them  another perspective whether they  continue the destruction or not is  completely up to them did you realize  that Saudi Arabia was destroying their  own history comment that down below also  drop a like if you hope that decides  rest in peace and of course consider  checking out our Discord Community to  suggest future video ideas and consider  subscribing to see more questions and  logically answered until then I'm Hari  and I'll see you guys on the next one  [Music]
 

